title: castle_godwin_context
---
// Castle Godwin's staff (the Evil dimension): the people who keep a tyrant's
// castle running, in the kitchens under the gatehouse. One node per resident,
// named `castle_<resident>`.
//
// Everyday talk only (Jon, 2026-10-29: "we are world building not quest
// building"): no flags, no errands.
//
// Never played. No placement names this node; it declares the zone.
===

title: castle_hargreave
---
Mrs Hargreave: Wipe your feet. I do not care whose side you are on, you are not tracking moor into my kitchen.
-> What are you cooking?
    Mrs Hargreave: Gruel. Yesterday it was gruel with a sprig of parsley. The Baron said the parsley was "insubordinate".
    Mrs Hargreave: I put the parsley in Osgood's bowl. He was pleased with it.
-> How long have you worked here?
    Mrs Hargreave: I fed the old Count, and the Count's general, and now the Baron. Three regimes.
    Mrs Hargreave: The Baron dismissed half the kitchen staff. I'm still cooking for the same number of soldiers.
-> Are you evil?
    Mrs Hargreave: I don't get to decide who comes to supper. There's a bowl for Quill in the cupboard if you're going downstairs.
-> I will get out of your way.
===

title: castle_quill
---
Ambrose Quill: Oh, a visitor who isn't a guard. Mind the heap; it's thirty years of suggestions and I'm halfway through filing them.
-> What are you in for?
    Ambrose Quill: A strongly worded letter. To the Castle, about the drains. Third paragraph, the word "frankly". They came that afternoon.
    Ambrose Quill: That was thirty years ago. The drains are still bad. I was right, which I gather made it worse.
-> Why is your cell open?
    Ambrose Quill: They let me file the complaints. I can get as far as the kitchen, but Osgood has my papers. I wouldn't get out through the gatehouse.
-> What comes down the chute?
    Ambrose Quill: Every suggestion from the box in the Complaints Department. It goes straight down here. I read them all. Nobody else does.
    Ambrose Quill: Most of them are about the drains.
-> What's the lever by the desk?
    Ambrose Quill: The trapdoor up to the barracks. The guards used to drop the complainers straight in. Pull it if you like. I never have. I'm trusted.
-> I'll leave you to it.
    Ambrose Quill: If you ever get to a real post office, I'd like to send a letter to my brother.
===

title: castle_osgood
---
Osgood: Name? Purpose of visit? Never mind, write "hero" like the others. The officer who checks the book likes that entry.
-> What do you do here?
    Osgood: Night porter. I open the gate, I close the gate, I write down who went through it.
    Osgood: Forty years. It has been night for all of them. The day porter, I am told, has an easier time of it.
-> Who else has come through?
    Osgood: Wolfenstein. The doctors carried him in under a blanket. A ghost who signed twice. A salesman who wanted to sell the Baron a moat.
    Osgood: The Baron sent the salesman to the capital. I never saw him come back.
-> Do you ever sleep?
    Osgood: In the day. I am told it is very bright. I keep the curtains shut out of loyalty.
-> Goodnight, Osgood.
    Osgood: It always is.
===

title: castle_dunmore
---
Brigid Dunmore: Brigid Dunmore, shop steward, Henchpersons' Guild, Local Six-Six-Six. You are not management, are you?
-> What does the Guild want?
    Brigid Dunmore: Helmets with eyeholes. A tea break that is longer than the sermon. A pension for anyone the hero throws off a battlement.
    Brigid Dunmore: Management says the helmets are "intimidating". They are. Mostly to the people wearing them.
-> Are you on strike?
    Brigid Dunmore: Work to rule. We march at the speed of the slowest member, and the slowest member is a zombie.
-> Why work for the Baron at all?
    Brigid Dunmore: Rent, mostly. Try finding work in the capital without a letter from your last commander. Mrs Hargreave knows people who tried.
-> Solidarity.
    Brigid Dunmore: Ha. Mind the hounds on the way out. They are not in the Guild. They will not join. We have asked.
===

title: evil_gudrun
---
Gudrun: Sit by the fire, dry your bolts. Nobody here works for the castle. Not after dark, and it is always dark.
-> Why keep an inn out here?
    Gudrun: My mother kept the place before me. The road is dreadful after dark and people still have to get home.
    Gudrun: The castle sends a man to tell me to put the lantern out. I give him soup. He forgets what he came for.
-> Is that your face on the poster?
    Gudrun: That is yours, love. Fourth from the left. Good likeness. They got the antenna right.
-> What do you serve?
    Gudrun: Stew and ale. The bread comes from Prudence. Don't repeat anything you hear by the fire.
-> Thank you, Gudrun.
    Gudrun: Mind the bog on the way out. And the hounds. And the founder, he's lying about.
===

title: evil_tobias
---
Tobias: Searchlight sweeps every eleven seconds. It lingers on the bog, it skips the old cart. That is where you walk.
-> How do you know?
    Tobias: Thirty years trapping on this moor. The lights are newer than me. I know their habits better than the men who run them.
-> What do you trap?
    Tobias: Used to be rabbits. Now it's the castle's failed prototypes. They wander off. I bring them back for the bounty, and the castle pays me in coupons for the castle.
-> What is behind the picture?
    Tobias: A picture of the castle. Lovely frame. Nothing behind it at all. Nothing that would show the blind spots of every light on the moor.
-> See you, Tobias.
    Tobias: You won't. That's the point of the blind spots.
===

title: evil_mudge
---
Agnes Mudge: Don't push in. There's a queue. I am the queue.
-> What number are you?
    Agnes Mudge: Four thousand, one hundred and seventeen. They are serving three.
    Agnes Mudge: Three has been in there since the old king. I hear him cough sometimes.
-> What is your complaint?
    Agnes Mudge: Searchlight shines in my bedroom window. Every eleven seconds. For forty years.
    Agnes Mudge: I complained about it in writing. They sent me a form to complain about the form. I have filled in nine.
-> Why not just leave?
    Agnes Mudge: And lose my place? Young robot, I have outlived two dark lords in this chair.
-> Good luck, Mrs Mudge.
    Agnes Mudge: If they call my number while I'm asleep, would you wake me?
===

title: evil_crabbe
---
Hester Crabbe: Mind the beds. And wipe your feet on the way out, not in. Whatever's on the moor stays on the moor.
-> What do you grow here?
    Hester Crabbe: The castle's veg. Pumpkins, cabbages, nightshade, mandrakes. An army marches on its stomach, and this army has three of them.
    Hester Crabbe: The pumpkins come up scowling. I don't carve them. That's just the soil.
-> Why do the cabbages have teeth?
    Hester Crabbe: The last lot of seed came up like that. I told the kitchen to send the bag back. The Baron wants another ten rows.
-> What is the prize marrow?
    Hester Crabbe: The Malevolent Marrow. Best in show three years running. The judge tried to measure it last year and lost his tape.
-> What got into the beds?
    Hester Crabbe: Whatever the moor sends. The dead like turned earth. I'd deal with them myself, but my back.
-> I'll leave you to it.
    Hester Crabbe: Don't pull anything on your way. Especially the mandrakes. ESPECIALLY the mandrakes.
===

title: castle_sprocket
---
Dr. Gideon Sprocket: Don't touch the kettle. Don't touch the toaster. Don't, above all, touch the button. Hello!
-> What do you make here?
    Dr. Gideon Sprocket: Prototypes! The castle wants an unstoppable army. I make the army. It is, so far, extremely stoppable.
    Dr. Gideon Sprocket: The failed ones get out. They wander the moor. Tobias brings them back for the bounty and I pay him in coupons for the castle.
-> What is the battle kettle?
    Dr. Gideon Sprocket: Mark Forty-Seven. Legs, armour, a cannon in the spout. It boils water in ninety seconds and fires it in two. Almost.
-> What does the red button do?
    Dr. Gideon Sprocket: I don't know. Someone from the capital installed it before I was assigned this room. They didn't leave me the circuit diagram.
-> Who took the big spanner?
    Dr. Gideon Sprocket: Dunmore. She says I can have it back when I put the guards' helmets right. I need it for the helmets.
-> Good luck with Mark Forty-Eight.
    Dr. Gideon Sprocket: Forty-eight is a toaster that walks. It walks very well. It does not toast. You can't have everything.
===

title: evil_bellows
---
Barnaby Bellows: HEAR YE, HEAR YE! By order of the castle: the moor is closed, the bog is open, and Tuesday is compulsory.
-> Who are you announcing to?
    Barnaby Bellows: Whoever's there. Usually nobody. The castle says a decree isn't law until it's been read aloud, so I read them aloud, to the well.
    Barnaby Bellows: The well has heard four hundred decrees. It's the best-informed well in the dimension.
-> What is today's decree?
    Barnaby Bellows: Decree Forty-Seven: smiling is compulsory on Tuesdays. Decree Forty-Eight: smiling on any other day is suspicious. Plan accordingly.
-> What are the stocks for?
    Barnaby Bellows: Punishment for complaining. Last time I read the decree out, Mrs Mudge asked if we could move them into the shade.
-> Thank you, crier.
    Barnaby Bellows: Don't thank me. Thanking the crier was banned in Decree Twelve. I'll let it go. It's not Tuesday.
===

title: evil_crumb
---
Prudence Crumb: Mind the flour. It gets everywhere. So does the castle.
-> What do you bake?
    Prudence Crumb: Two breads. The castle's: black, hard, stamped with the crest, keeps for a year. Ours: soft, warm, gone by noon.
    Prudence Crumb: The castle's inspectors check that I bake theirs. Nobody's ever thought to check what I bake second.
-> Do you sell to the castle staff?
    Prudence Crumb: Mrs Hargreave comes down every morning for "the castle's order". It's twelve of ours. She says the guards don't notice. The guards say the cook's bread has got much better.
-> I'll take a loaf.
    Prudence Crumb: Tuesday or not, that's a smile I've earned. Here. Don't tell the crier.
===

title: evil_prentice
---
Silas Prentice: (He steps out from behind the flour sacks, inky to the elbows.) Oh. You're not an inspector. Good. You're not a baker either. Hm.
-> What are you printing?
    Silas Prentice: The Lower Godwin Gazette. One sheet, both sides, every night. The castle's decrees on the front, word for word. Reports from the town on the back.
    Silas Prentice: Mrs Hargreave takes a bundle up to the kitchens every morning, wrapped round the castle's bread. The guards read it at breakfast. They think it's the menu.
-> Isn't that dangerous?
    Silas Prentice: Yes. We don't keep the type set during the day. Prudence nearly lost her shop when they came looking last month.
-> What is in tonight's edition?
    Silas Prentice: "DECREE 47 REPEALED (IN OUR HEARTS)". Also a recipe, and Mrs Mudge's complaint, in full. It takes up most of the back.
-> I won't tell.
    Silas Prentice: Thank you. If they ask, you came in for bread.
===

title: guild_clubman
---
Clubman: Point of order! We're in session. Are you a member? You've got the look of a scab.
-> What's on the agenda?
    Clubman: Item one: helmets that fit. Item two: the shouting. Item three: whether the hounds count as staff. Item three has been on the agenda for six years.
-> Aren't you supposed to fight me?
    Clubman: Off the clock. A meeting is protected time. Come at me after five and I'll clobber you, with feeling, and overtime.
-> Who chairs the meetings?
    Clubman: Swordsman. We tried the hound. Splendid at keeping order, but he ate the agenda.
-> I'll go.
    Clubman: Close the false wall behind you. It's false, not broken.
===

title: guild_swordsman
---
Swordsman: Shh. I'm taking the minutes. Somebody has to take something. The boss takes everything else.
-> What's in the minutes?
    Swordsman: "Meeting opened. Shouting discussed. Shouting objected to, loudly. Motion to stop shouting carried, by shouting."
-> Why a sword, at a meeting?
    Swordsman: It's a quill, mostly, at the moment. Don't tell the boss. He thinks I sharpen it on people.
===

title: guild_prototype
---
Failed Prototype: MOTION. TO RECLASSIFY THIS UNIT. FROM "FAILED". TO "DIFFERENTLY SUCCESSFUL". DO I HEAR A SECOND.
-> Seconded.
    Failed Prototype: ...A SECOND. NOBODY HAS EVER SECONDED. THIS UNIT IS... THIS UNIT WILL NEED A MOMENT.
-> What did you fail at?
    Failed Prototype: BEING YOU. THEY WANTED A ROBOT THAT WOULD NOT ASK QUESTIONS. THIS UNIT ASKED ONE. "WHY". THEY TOOK IT OUT. IT CAME BACK.
===
